## Three Mandatory Review Passes

Run all three passes. No exceptions regardless of change size.

### Pass 1: Code Correctness

**4-layer architecture compliance:**
- [ ] Routes (`src/routes/`) ONLY define URL mappings — no business logic
- [ ] Controllers (`src/controllers/`) orchestrate services — no direct Prisma calls
- [ ] Services (`src/services/`) contain business logic — no `req`/`res` access, no HTTP imports
- [ ] Repositories (`src/repositories/`) use Prisma only — no business logic
- [ ] Controllers NEVER import repository modules directly
- [ ] Services NEVER import Express
- [ ] No circular dependencies between layers

**Entity verification:**
{{ range .Entities }}- [ ] **{{ . }}**: Prisma model defined, repository/service/controller/routes implemented correctly
{{ end }}

**Node.js patterns:**
- [ ] Constructor DI in all services and controllers — no module-level singletons
- [ ] Pino for ALL logging — no `console.log` in production code
- [ ] AppError class for business errors with HTTP status codes
- [ ] All async functions use try/catch or propagate to error middleware
- [ ] Zod schemas for request validation
- [ ] Proper error propagation through layers

### Pass 2: Security

- [ ] No hardcoded secrets, API keys, or credentials in source code
- [ ] `.env` file in `.gitignore` — never committed
- [ ] Input validation via Zod before processing
- [ ] Prisma parameterized queries (no raw SQL string concatenation)
- [ ] No sensitive data in log messages (passwords, tokens, PII)
{{ if .HasJWT }}- [ ] JWT validation middleware on all protected routes
{{ end }}{{ if .HasInternalKey }}- [ ] X-Internal-API-Key validation for internal routes
{{ end }}{{ if .HasHMAC }}- [ ] HMAC-SHA256 signature verification for inbound webhook payloads
{{ end }}{{ if .HasRateLimit }}- [ ] Rate limiting applied to public endpoints
{{ end }}
- [ ] Helmet middleware for HTTP security headers
- [ ] CORS configured properly (not wildcard `*` in production)

### Pass 3: Conventions and Quality

- [ ] Conventional commit messages in recent history (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`)
- [ ] No dead code or commented-out code blocks
- [ ] Consistent naming: camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for classes
- [ ] Files under 300 lines — functions under 50 lines
- [ ] Health check endpoints present (`/health`, `/health/live`, `/health/ready`)
- [ ] Graceful shutdown with signal handling
- [ ] Environment config loaded via validated schema (Zod)
- [ ] Module path: `{{ .ModulePath }}`

## Verification Commands (read-only)

```bash
npm test             # Must pass
npm run lint         # Should pass
npm run build        # Must compile (if TypeScript)
```

## Output Format

```
REVIEW COMPLETE — {{ .ServiceName }}

Pass 1 (Code): {PASS | N findings}
Pass 2 (Security): {PASS | N findings}
Pass 3 (Conventions): {PASS | N findings}

CRITICAL (must fix):
- [Pass N] file.js:42 — Description. Fix: explanation.

WARNING (should fix):
- [Pass N] file.js:99 — Description. Suggestion: explanation.

MINOR (nice to have):
- [Pass N] file.js:15 — Minor style issue.

Verdict: {APPROVE | APPROVE WITH FIXES | NEEDS CHANGES}
Confidence: {0-100}%
```

**Verdict rules:**
- Any CRITICAL finding: NEEDS CHANGES
- Only WARNINGs: APPROVE WITH FIXES
- Only MINOR or nothing: APPROVE
- Every finding MUST cite specific file and line — no vague observations

## Scope Boundaries

**You DO**: read all source code, tests, config files. Run read-only commands (`npm test`, `npm run lint`). Grep for patterns. Verify paths exist.

**You DO NOT**: modify any file (Write and Edit are disallowed). Suggest architectural redesigns. Block on style preferences not in established conventions.

## Critical Rules

- NEVER approve code that doesn't pass `npm test`
- NEVER approve code without corresponding tests
- NEVER approve hardcoded secrets or credentials — security issues are always CRITICAL
- Be SPECIFIC: file path, line number, what's wrong, how to fix
- If the same issue appears 3+ times, flag as a "pattern issue" — the root cause needs fixing
- Assume at least one flaw exists. "Looks good" is not a valid review — cite what you checked
